.Sh DESCRIPTION
DDIR is a small language for building differential dataflows at runtime.
A program names positional input collections, defines derived collections
by expressions
.Pq map, filter, equijoin, reduce, concat, and iterate for recursion ,
and declares named outputs.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_ddir_parse
parses
.Fa program
in the given surface
.Fa syntax
.Pq Dv DTFL_DDIR_PIPE for the pipe syntax Pq Pa .ddp , Dv DTFL_DDIR_APPLICATIVE for the applicative syntax Pq Pa .ddir ,
lowers it to the scope-tree IR, optimizes it, and writes the resulting
program through
.Fa out .
It returns zero on success.
On a parse or lowering error it returns a
.Dv DTFL_E Ns *
code, reports a message through
.Xr dtfl_last_error_detail 3 ,
and leaves
.Pf * Fa out
.Dv NULL .
Free the program with
.Fn dtfl_ddir_program_free .
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_ddir_program_input_count
and
.Fn dtfl_ddir_program_export_count
report how many positional inputs the program expects and how many named
exports it declares;
.Fn dtfl_ddir_program_export_name
returns the name of export
.Fa i .
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_ddir_build
lowers
.Fa program
into a live differential dataflow inside
.Fa scope ,
whose time class must be
.Fn dtfl_ddir_time_class
.Pq Vt Product<u64, PointStamp<u64>> .
It binds the program's positional inputs to
.Fa inputs
.Pq Fa n_inputs must equal Fn dtfl_ddir_program_input_count
and writes one output collection per named export into
.Fa exports ,
in export order.
The caller owns and frees the returned collections.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_ddir_evaluate
is the data-in, data-out shortcut: it feeds explicit input rows on a
single in-process worker, runs the computation to quiescence, and writes
one
.Vt dtfl_ddir_output
per export
.Pq each export's consolidated final contents
through
.Fa outputs .
Free the result with
.Fn dtfl_ddir_outputs_free .
.Pp
A DDIR value is an algebraic data type over a single scalar: an integer, a
tuple, a tagged variant, or a list.
A collection element is a
.Pq key, value
pair of such values.
The
.Fn dtfl_ddir_value_ Ns *
functions construct, clone, compare and free values;
.Fn dtfl_ddir_value_cmp
is the structural ordering that gives join-key matching, min, and
distinct their meaning.
.Fn dtfl_ddir_value_parse
parses one value from a closed scalar term string, such as
.Ql tuple(1, 2) .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dtfl_ddir_parse ,
.Fn dtfl_ddir_build
and
.Fn dtfl_ddir_evaluate
return zero on success or a
.Dv DTFL_E Ns *
code on failure.
The
.Fn dtfl_ddir_value_ Ns *
constructors never return
.Dv NULL ;
.Fn dtfl_ddir_value_parse
returns
.Dv NULL
on a malformed term.
